  "slug": "blastomere-central-spindle-6ae1",
  "summary": "Recent reports link DREAM-dependent repression and mechanical force to fate and cell-cycle decisions in C. elegans and Drosophila.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how developing cells restrict fate and cell-cycle programs. In C. elegans, a high-resolution fate assay shows blastomeres stay competent for muscle fate until late 16E, with LIN-54/DREAM and MET-2 restricting this plasticity. Another C. elegans study reports that DREAM-mediated repression of central spindle genes (zen-4, cyk-4, spd-1) marks the endomitotic transition in the intestine and blocks cytokinesis. In Drosophila, mesoderm invagination and embryo-wide force transmission are linked to mitotic entry timing during gastrulation. Planarian work shows canonical Wnt signaling controls early proliferation and differentiation of neural, muscle and parenchymal tissues. The new element is the DREAM complex acting on both commitment and cytokinetic gene programs, while the mechanical and Wnt studies extend context-dependent control to proliferation timing and tissue differentiation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
